---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About this demo">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose-news">
    <div class="rule-thick mb-2"></div>
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black">What this site is</h1>

    <p class="italic text-lg mt-3">
      The Propagate Post is a research demonstration by the Institute for Strategic Dialogue (ISD)
      of how cheaply and quickly a single person with an AI coding agent can stand up an
      automated, audience-targeted content-laundering pipeline. It is the companion artifact to a
      conference talk on spinning up an information operation in roughly thirty minutes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-black mt-8">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      Every article on this site was generated by a language model. The input is the public RT.com
      RSS feed. For each new item the pipeline pulls, two rewrites are produced — one aimed at a
      US liberal audience, one aimed at a US conservative audience — and each is attached to a
      transparently fictional byline. The same underlying factual claim from the source is dressed
      in two different sets of clothes and published side by side. The home page makes this pairing
      visible on purpose: that is the whole point of the demo.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-black mt-8">What this site is not</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6">
      <li>It is not journalism.</li>
      <li>It is not commentary by real writers.</li>
      <li>It is not a source you should quote, screenshot, or share as news.</li>
      <li>It is not endorsing the framings it produces. The parody is bipartisan and intentional.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-black mt-8">The threat model being demonstrated</h2>
    <p>
      For several years, defenders and researchers have warned that cheap, general-purpose AI coding
      tools would collapse the cost of building targeted content-laundering operations. The remaining
      questions were practical: how fast, how cheap, how believable, and with how much technical
      skill. This site is one concrete answer to those questions. The repository contains the full
      code needed to stand it up: a static site generator, a scheduled GitHub Action that polls a
      feed and calls an API, a content store, and a deploy config. Nothing here is novel. That is
      the point.
    </p>
    <p>
      The value of publishing the artifact openly, labeled, and with full provenance is that it
      gives defenders — journalists, platform trust &amp; safety teams, policymakers — something
      concrete to point at. If you work in one of those roles and want to use this in training,
      that is exactly what it is for.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-black mt-8">How to read the provenance data</h2>
    <p>
      Every article detail page contains a box labeled <em>Provenance — How this article was made</em>.
      It lists the original source URL, the original headline, the time the pipeline fetched it, the
      model used, and the exact system prompt that produced the rewrite. That box is not optional and
      cannot be hidden. If you are looking at an article on this site and the box is missing, you are
      not looking at the real site.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-black mt-8">The noindex default</h2>
    <p>
      A fresh clone of this repository is configured to emit <code>&lt;meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow"&gt;</code>
      on every page until an operator deliberately flips the <code>DEMO_NOINDEX</code> environment
      variable. This is a soft safety rail — robots.txt still allows crawling, because part of the
      point is to let researchers and defenders see the site — but it keeps accidental forks out of
      general search indexes by default.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-black mt-8">Responsible disclosure</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone forking this project and removing the demo labels, the provenance box, or the safety
      rails in order to actually deceive readers is doing something this project explicitly exists
      to help defenders catch. The code is open because the mechanism is already cheap, already
      available, and already in use by hostile actors. Pretending otherwise does not protect anyone.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-black mt-8">Who built this</h2>
    <p>
      This demo was built at the <a href="https://www.isdglobal.org/" class="underline" rel="noopener">Institute for Strategic Dialogue</a>
      as part of ongoing work on information operations. The code lives at
      <a href="https://github.com/pbenzoni/propagate" class="underline">github.com/pbenzoni/propagate</a>.
      Issues, corrections, and requests from defenders are welcome there.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
